      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Though uncommon, osteoporosis can occur in pregnancy or shortly after delivery. The most common feature is back pain, often severely disabling. Suspect osteoporosis if pain of sudden onset in the upper lumbar or thoracic spine is not relieved by simple analgesia, or if there is a noticeable loss of height. X-rays reveal low bone density and fractures of the vertebrae. 70% of cases occur in first pregnancies. Recurrence is unusual. Most cases resolve spontaneously; a minority cause disability lasting months or years. If osteoporosis is diagnosed, breast feeding should be discouraged because of its effect on bone mineral density. Anyone who has had osteoporosis of pregnancy is at risk of postmenopausal osteoporosis and should take medical advice."},
